[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one bug and one fix. The reports merged into it say otherwise.
The desk merged them on the subject line alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)),
so reports with the same words but a different cause landed in the same ticket.

<Callout>
The queued fix covers the first problem below. The other two stay broken after it ships.
</Callout>

## The split

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

The export repeats some rows; one id counts once. A report's problem comes from its tenant, not from
what intake guessed from the browser (`causes.ts`).

## 1 · `parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first

Tenants that write the day first get their dates flipped. This is the bug #4471 names. Most of the reports
are this one. The queued fix belongs to this problem only.

## 2 · `hint`: booking-v3 tells people to type month first

Through booking-v3's form, people at day-first tenants type what the hint prints under the date field,
`MM/DD/YYYY`, and the form reads it their way. The parser is not at fault; the hint is.
Intake tagged many of these `parser` because the symptom looks the same.

<Callout>
Fixing the parser alone makes this worse: once the parser reads day first, people who typed month first
because of the hint will be flipped the other way.
</Callout>

## 3 · `one-day`: bookings move by one day, not a swap

The desk already read these as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`). A swap of day and month cannot give
"Tuesday became Monday", or the same meeting landing on two different days for two time zones. This looks
like a time zone problem. It needs its own ticket and its own owner.

## What to do

- Keep #4471 for problem 1 and its fix.
- Open a ticket for problem 2 and one for problem 3, each with its own count.
- Re-tag intake: the browser guess disagrees with the cause too often to trust.
- Revisit decision 0001: merging on the subject line alone is how three problems became one ticket.

